bibliograph-worker (0.6.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * 0.6.0: less work per ingest, per sweep and per publish; a search index.

    - `0023`: the `edition_shape` and `edition_publisher_list` refreshes are
      statement-level triggers with transition tables, so a candidate writing
      N claims aggregates once per edition instead of N times (O(N²) → O(N)),
      and an unchanged aggregate writes no row version.
    - `0024`: `pds_outbox_claim_idx` matches `claimBatch`'s collection filter
      and its `(next_at, updated_at, uri)` order.
    - `0025`: `catalog_enqueue_marks` moves into the migrations — it was
      created at worker boot, so the schema checks could not see it and a fresh
      database lacked it until the worker started. `catalog.marks.prune`
      deletes marks older than 24 h, hourly.
    - `0026`: the Goodreads page cache moves from process memory into
      Postgres, with the same TTL as the other provider caches.
    - `0027`: `book_search`, one tsvector per book (title A, contributors B,
      shape description C, identifier values D) with a GIN index, maintained by
      statement-level triggers on the four contributing tables. The edge's free
      text search reads it; the backfill runs once (2m34s for 234k books) and
      every replay inserts nothing.
    - Ingest batches identifier claims and contributor identities with
      `unnest` (a 3-identifier, 4-contributor candidate: 21 statements → 11);
      each sweep claims its whole batch in one statement; publish settles a
      drained batch in at most six statements; Bookhive commits per record
      instead of per page.
    - ISBNdb quota is reserved in blocks of ten, so the hot `(provider, day)`
      row takes one write per ten calls.

