bibliograph-worker (0.2.26-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * 0.2.26: the Bookhive import reads a page's hive ids once and commits a
    page once, instead of committing every record.

    - `knownEditions` resolved a page's hive ids through
      `edition_uri IN (SELECT …)`. The planner estimates `value = ANY($1)` at
      four rows whatever the array holds — measured on 500 ids: 4 planned, 500
      actual — and that underestimate nested-looped 1 265 times in the joins
      below. Naming the set in a `MATERIALIZED` CTE builds it once at its real
      cardinality: a 500-id page drops from 1 025 ms to 300 ms (plan 129 ms →
      6 ms), the real 100-id page from 46.7 ms to 43.6 ms. `DISTINCT` keeps one
      row per edition, and the old and new forms returned 253 rows with an
      identical md5 over the ordered result set for one page.
    - `createIngest` splits into `prepare` (validation, no connection),
      `ingestOn` (writes on a caller-owned client) and the unchanged public
      `ingest`, so the import wraps a page in one `BEGIN`/`COMMIT` with a
      savepoint per record. A record that throws rolls back to its own
      savepoint: the page lands the rest and counts one failure, which is what
      the per-record loop promised. A shutdown mid-page commits what it wrote.
      A page now pays one commit instead of ~100; the gain lands on the cold
      backfill (234 000 ingests ≈ 70 s of commit overhead).
    - The ESM changes; `sql/` does not — the newest migration is still
      `0019-entity-fanout-guard.sql`, so installing this package re-applies the
      schema as a no-op.

