 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Sun, 15 Nov 2026 01:19:19 +0000

bibliograph-worker (0.2.18-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * 0.2.18 release: a refused cover URL is terminal, and downloads are counted.

    - `downloadCover` treats an HTTP 4xx (other than 408/429) as a
      `CoverRejectedError`: a Google Books `books/content` URL that answers 403
      answers the same way tomorrow, so retrying it only spent provider
      requests. Those jobs used to exhaust their five attempts and leave the
      ledger row `inflight`, so the same URL was re-attempted a week later.
      408/429/5xx and transport failures stay retryable, and every error now
      carries the HTTP `status` that produced it.
    - New `bibliograph_cover_downloads_total{result,status}` counter
      (`result` = `cached` | `rejected` | `retry`, `status` = the HTTP status or
      `none`) on the worker scrape. Cover download failures were previously
      only in the journal, which is why the 403s were invisible to alerting.
      The counter is process-local, so it resets on restart, like the provider
      counters beside it.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Sat, 14 Nov 2026 19:19:57 +0000

bibliograph-worker (0.2.17-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * 0.2.17 release: covers are cached on disk, and a format carries its unit.

    - 0012-edition-cover-files.sql adds `edition_cover_files` (the cached image
      per edition, keyed with the provider URL it came from) and gives
      `edition_cover_fetches` the same `source_url` plus a terminal `rejected`
      outcome, so a bad URL is not retried for a week.
    - `catalog.covers` downloads the bytes, sniffs the image type, rejects
      anything under 4 KiB or not an image (nulling `cover_url` and recording
      the rejection) and writes through a temp file plus rename. A transport or
      provider failure still throws, so graphile retries instead of filing an
      absence. OpenLibrary's 43-byte transparent GIF is what this fixes.
    - `catalog.covers.sweep` gains a backfill pass (source URL present, bytes
      not cached) ahead of the never-attempted and retry passes and batches
      500/5000: it only enqueues, while `downloadCover`'s gate paces the
      fetches.
    - Published records advertise `<COVERS_PUBLIC_BASE_URL>/api/covers/<uri>`
      once the bytes are cached, and the provider URL until then.
    - `COVERS_CACHE_DIR` is configuration only: unset disables the cache with a
      boot log instead of guessing a writable path. The unit's
      `CacheDirectory=bibliograph-covers` creates and owns the directory.
    - 0013-edition-identifier-extent.sql makes the identifier's format and its
      size one `extent` claim (`{"format":"Hardcover","pages":320}`,
      `{"format":"Audiobook","durationSeconds":36000}`). `format` becomes a
      STORED generated column and `pages` / `duration_seconds` generated ones, so
      a row can no longer claim a paperback and an audio duration at once. A
      BEFORE trigger prunes the unit the format does not use, and
      `edition_identifier_shape` publishes only the winning identifier's unit.
    - Ingest writes `extent`, merged over the stored claim, so providers keep
      filling gaps only.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Sat, 14 Nov 2026 19:04:07 +0000

bibliograph-worker (0.2.16-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * 0.2.16 release: duration is an identifier claim, and covers are backfilled.

    - 0010-edition-identifier-duration.sql moves `duration_seconds` off
      `editions` onto `edition_identifier_info`, backfilling every identifier
      with its edition's value and appending `duration_seconds` to
      `edition_identifier_shape`; the drop is guarded, so re-applying the file
      is a no-op. Ingest writes the claim and publishing reads the shape view,
      so the record's `durationSeconds` is the rank union of the identifiers
      (an abridged and an unabridged ISBN keep their own value).
    - 0007/0008 stay replay-safe against that drop: the claim table gains the
      column with `ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS`, and `appview_book` reads
      `duration_seconds` from the shape view instead of `editions`.
    - 0009-edition-cover-fetches.sql adds the `edition_cover_fetches` ledger, a
      partial index over coverless editions and
      `catalog_enqueue_cover_fetch`, the claim-and-enqueue entry point shared by
      the edge and the sweep.
    - `catalog.covers` backfills one edition's cover from OpenLibrary
      (`coverForEdition`: the ISBN record, an `OL…M` edition document, or the
      work's edition list) and then Google Books for an ISBN. A "no cover"
      answer is recorded, a provider outage rethrows so the job retries, and the
      edition update only fires while `cover_url` is still empty.
    - `catalog.covers.sweep` runs every five minutes in batches of 100 (500 at
      most): never-attempted editions first, then the seven-day retry pass.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Sat, 14 Nov 2026 16:52:20 +0000

bibliograph-worker (0.2.15-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * 0.2.15 release: edition shape claims and publisher links move onto the
    identifier.

    - 0007-edition-identifier-info.sql moves `language`, `published_date`,
      `format`, `pages` and `description` off `editions` onto the new
      `edition_identifier_info` table (one claim row per identifier, cascading
      from `edition_identifiers`), backfills every identifier with its edition's
      current values, and drops the five columns. `edition_identifier_shape`
      unions the claims back into the edition's public shape in identifier order
      (isbn13, isbn10, asin, then any other issuer).
    - 0008-edition-identifier-publishers.sql replaces `edition_publishers` with
      `edition_identifier_publishers` (FK to `publishers`, cascading from
      `edition_identifiers`), backfills each edition's links onto every
      identifier, and drops the old table. `edition_publishers_shape` orders the
      links per edition; the record publishes its head as `publisher` and the
      whole list as `publishers`.
    - Ingest writes the candidate's claims and its publisher onto every
      identifier it supplied, filling only empty claim fields. Publishing reads
      both shape views, so `appview_book` keeps its column list and HappyView
      needs no change.
    - `editions-missing-*` reports now ask whether any identifier records the
      claim, and `edition-identifier-info` enumerates identifiers with their
      claims and linked publisher names.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Sat, 14 Nov 2026 16:18:36 +0000

bibliograph-worker (0.2.14-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * 0.2.14 release: ISBNdb as a metered genre source and a soft quota gauge.

    - `catalog.genres` now falls back to ISBNdb when Goodreads and OpenLibrary
      both return nothing: `genresForEdition` reads the ISBN's book record and
      its `subjects` list, which ISBNdb marks deprecated, so it is a last
      resort and not a primary source. It goes through the provider's metered
      path — a cached `/book/{isbn}` (seven days) costs no daily call, a miss
      reserves one from `provider_daily_usage` — and a refused quota answers
      `undefined`, so the ledger row is left unmarked instead of recording "no
      genres". `mergeGenres` appends the subject sources alphabetically after
      Goodreads' relevance order, and 0006-genre-sources.sql widens the
      `edition_genres.source` CHECK to accept `isbndb`.
    - The Prometheus exposition splits the ISBNdb quota into a hard limit and a
      soft one: `bibliograph_isbndb_daily_limit` (5000, what `reserveDailyCall`
      enforces) and `bibliograph_isbndb_daily_soft_limit` (95% of it — 4750 —
      where an alert should fire), with `bibliograph_isbndb_calls_remaining`
      and `bibliograph_isbndb_calls_remaining_soft` alongside them.
      `renderMetrics` now defaults the limit instead of rendering 0.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Sat, 14 Nov 2026 14:53:18 +0000

bibliograph-worker (0.2.13-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * 0.2.13 release: contributor merge support and a cheaper genre sweep.

    - One contributor (or publisher) can legitimately hold two credits on one
      edition — author and translator. `pds_enqueue_contributor_editions` and
      `pds_enqueue_publisher_editions` (0001) joined the link table by entity id
      and inserted one outbox row per credit position, so the second insert
      made `ON CONFLICT` fail with `cannot affect row a second time` (21000) and
      rolled back the whole edit. Both now select `DISTINCT` editions. The web
      editor's new contributor merge creates exactly that state, which is how
      the defect surfaced; the definitions are `CREATE OR REPLACE`, so applying
      the schema repairs an existing database.
    - `catalog.genres.sweep` no longer proves an empty fresh set with a set
      difference over the whole catalog: two index-only counts short-circuit it
      when every edition already has a ledger row. The retry pass reads the
      ledger directly and orders by its clock, and
      `edition_genre_fetches_retry_idx` (0005) is partial on
      `outcome IS DISTINCT FROM 'found'` so already-answered editions leave the
      walk, replacing the per-row join back to `edition_genres`.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Sat, 14 Nov 2026 08:31:35 +0000

bibliograph-worker (0.2.12-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * 0.2.12 release: contributor biographies and edition genres.

    - Persist the provider biography of each credited contributor on ingest.
      OpenLibrary author detail is now read for every keyed author of an
      edition lookup — the name and the biography — still bounded to ten per
      lookup and shared across a work's editions. The new
      `describeContributors` provider method fills a missing bio for the admin
      transport. `linkContributors` writes `contributors.description`, clipped
      to the record's 1024-grapheme / 8192-byte lexicon bounds, and fills it
      only when the row has none, so an operator-curated bio is never
      replaced. `/ingest` and `/publisher-import` enrich before writing;
      `catalog.search` is unchanged, so a 50-candidate run cannot multiply
      upstream calls. No backfill: existing rows gain a bio the next time an
      edition crediting them is ingested.
    - Add edition genres (0005-genres.sql): a curated `genres` vocabulary, the
      `edition_genres` links and an `edition_genre_fetches` claim ledger, plus
      `catalog_enqueue_genre_fetch` as the single enqueue point shared by the
      edge and the worker. The new `catalog.genres` task hydrates one edition
      from OpenLibrary subjects and Goodreads book-page genres, mapped to
      canonical names before storage; `catalog.genres.sweep` walks ungenred
      editions every ten minutes and once at worker boot. A label with no
      canonical mapping is dropped rather than stored raw. Genres are
      edge-only and are not part of the published edition record.
    - Provider reads: OpenLibrary `subjectsFromWork` (with one redirect hop
      followed for a merged work key) and Goodreads `genresForEdition`, which
      resolves by Goodreads id or ISBN and never falls back to a title search.
      A WAF challenge still propagates as a provider failure, not an empty
      answer.

